1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,842
CHAPTER 4 — THE COMPUTER THAT DIDN'T EXIST

2
00:00:04,192 --> 00:00:11,346
In 1977, at a major computer fair in San Francisco, Apple unveiled the Apple II.

3
00:00:11,546 --> 00:00:23,546
And Wozniak, in secret, handed out thousands of brochures for a spectacular machine: the Zaltair. It didn't exist. It was a parody of the Altair.

4
00:00:23,746 --> 00:00:32,977
Jobs fell for it. He checked the comparison chart and felt proud: the Apple II held its own. Wozniak told him the truth years later.

5
00:00:33,177 --> 00:00:44,638
Other surprises weren't on purpose. In 1980 came the Apple III, with no fan, as Jobs wanted. From the heat, chips popped out of their sockets.

6
00:00:44,838 --> 00:00:53,915
According to accounts from that era, some customers were advised to lift it a few inches and drop it. That pushed the chips back into place.

7
00:00:54,865 --> 00:00:59,096
Tech support fit into a single word: drop it.
